## Method and reasoning
An interval’s final multiplication splits after some matrix k. Independently optimize both halves, then add d[l]·d[k+1]·d[r+1] for zero-based matrix indices. Fill intervals in increasing length. Single matrices have zero cost. Use 64 bits for the dimension product and total.

## Walk through the sample
Multiplying the first pair costs 10 × 30 × 5 = 1500, then the final product costs 10 × 5 × 60 = 3000. The opposite grouping costs 9000 + 18000. Both produce a 10 × 60 matrix.

## Complexity
Time O(n³), space O(n²).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> d(n + 1);
    for (auto &x : d)
        cin >> x;
    vector<vector<long long>> dp(n, vector<long long>(n));
    for (int len = 2; len <= n; ++len)
        for (int l = 0; l + len <= n; ++l) {
            int r = l + len - 1;
            dp[l][r] = LLONG_MAX;
            for (int k = l; k < r; ++k)
                dp[l][r] =
                    min(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + dp[k + 1][r] + d[l] * d[k + 1] * d[r + 1]);
        }
    cout << dp[0][n - 1] << '\n';
}
